WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.375
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Hey, everyone.

00:00:01.375 --> 00:00:03.450
I am Priyanka Vergadia,
and you are watching

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.100
"Deconstructing Chatbots."

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.787
In today's episode, we will
explore entities further.

00:00:08.787 --> 00:00:11.649
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:18.320
From the second
and third episodes,

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.130
you might remember that entities
are Dialogflow's mechanism

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:27.710
for identifying and extracting
useful data from user's input.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:29.660
If you've not checked
out the episode

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.970
on "Getting Started
with Dialogflow,"

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.190
please do so for more context.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.370
Just scroll up to the
second episode on the right,

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.280
or click in the link
and description below.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.290
There are three types of
entities-- system entities,

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.430
developer entities,
and session entities.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.910
Now you might remember from our
appointment scheduler chatbot

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:53.120
when we said need an appointment
for 4:00 PM tomorrow,

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.040
the date and time
were automatically

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.940
extracted as system
entities date and time.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.650
It was all automatic.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:02.990
As you saw, system
entities allow

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:07.070
agents to extract information
about a wide range of concepts

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.830
without any additional
configuration.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:14.270
Data like address, emails,
currency, phone numbers

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:17.810
are some of the common
examples of system entities.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.140
Now so far, we have been
addressing our appointment

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.240
scheduler in a generic manner.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.990
Let's say our
scheduler is for DMV.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:30.710
We click on Entities
and create a new entity.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:34.780
Let's call this
Appointment Type.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.350
Let's think about the types
of services DMV offers.

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.410
Most people go to the DMV
for a drivers license,

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:45.380
maybe vehicle registration,
also driving tests.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:48.610
So in our scenario, let's stick
to the two main services--

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:51.370
drivers license and
vehicle registration

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:53.650
are obviously the
two main ones that I

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.960
can think of that DMV offers.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.470
So let's create those
two as entities.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:03.370
You can add more rows with
more services as entity types.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:04.960
Additionally, you
can check the box

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.210
to add more entities
automatically.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.910
When an entity is not
available in the entity list,

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.150
Dialogflow identifies that
this is an entity based

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:16.030
on other training phrases
and adds that new entity

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:17.900
automatically to the list.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.860
You can also upload or
download entities in bulk

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:24.260
in CSV or JSON format.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:27.190
Now let's add more intents to
accommodate the appointment

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:29.080
type entity in our inputs.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.960
When you add Set an Appointment
for Driver's License for 3:00

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:36.670
PM tomorrow, driver's license is
identified as developer entity

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.760
that we just set up, while
time and date are obviously

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.710
system entities.

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:45.670
Add a few more training
phrases like these.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.000
Now remember slot filling
from episode three

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.220
when we built our
appointment scheduler?

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.020
To book an accurate
appointment, we

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.050
needed to make appointment
type a required field

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.760
just like time and date.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.060
Let's define the right prompt
for when a user does not

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.980
provide us the appointment type.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.220
Now while we are at it,
let's modify our response

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.970
to include the appointment type
along with the date and time

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.100
to make it look more complete.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.280
When done, don't forget
to save your changes.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.600
And then, let's test our updates
now with some sample inputs.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.780
The first example,
let's say, user only

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.730
gives us the appointment type.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:28.110
Well, slot filling kicks in and
asks for the time and the date

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.500
when they want to come in.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:31.560
All looks good.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.180
We get the
appointment scheduled.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:38.900
In the second example,
let's say, a user says,

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:40.800
I need an appointment
for 4:00 PM tomorrow

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.850
without providing us
the appointment type.

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:47.090
Again, our slot filling
kicks in and prompts the user

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:50.100
to provide us the
service information.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:52.100
They say driver's
license, and we

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:56.570
provide them a response with
their appointment confirmation.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:59.360
In our third and last
example, let's say

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.790
the user gave us
all three things

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:05.240
we needed in just one shot--
set an appointment for vehicle

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.430
registration at
2:00 PM tomorrow.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:09.740
Our bot has everything
it needs, so it

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:14.390
sends a confirmation response
back to the user right away.

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:18.050
Now the third type of
entity is session entity.

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:21.320
These are defined at
session ID level, which

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.290
allows for a specific
entity to be tied

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:27.930
to a user and their session.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:31.040
Let's take an example to
understand this better.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.130
Our agent had appointment
type as entity,

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:36.650
which includes drivers
license and vehicle

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:39.050
registration at this point.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:42.410
That entity could be
updated to include a state

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:45.140
ID or a motorcycle
test depending

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.230
on the information your
agent collects from the user

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:50.630
during the conversation.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:55.730
The updated session entity will
have state ID or motorcycle

00:04:55.730 --> 00:05:00.040
test entry for rest of the
conversation with your user.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.030
Well, try this out
on your own, and let

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:04.720
me know how it goes
in the comments below.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.890
Remember, you will
create, manage, and update

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:11.240
session entities using the API.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:13.220
That was a lot, so
let's summarize.

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:16.160
Today, we looked at the
three types of entities--

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.850
system entity, developer
entity, and session entities.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.220
Then, we went hands
on and learned

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:25.310
how to set up these entities
in our appointment scheduler

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:26.720
chatbot.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.570
Don't miss the next episode
of "Deconstructing Chatbots"

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:32.330
because we are going to
integrate our appointment

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:35.270
scheduler with Twilio
Messaging Service.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:38.620
[MUSIC PLAYING]

